00:15:14.664 --> 00:15:15.624
Kenzo: Hmm. Great question.

00:15:16.824 --> 00:15:20.124
Wellby: So, uh, big walls to get over.

00:15:20.124 --> 00:15:23.904
Wellby: It's probably worth everyone having, like, rope or grappling hooks.

00:15:23.904 --> 00:15:28.304
Wellby: On the other side, a field of, like, giant attack vines.

00:15:28.304 --> 00:15:29.104
Seeker: Oh, God.

00:15:29.104 --> 00:15:33.124
Wellby: So there's no, there's not, like, like, Talgar Roundup or, like...

00:15:33.124 --> 00:15:35.244
Seeker: [laughs] Flamethrower.

00:15:35.244 --> 00:15:36.204
DM: Fireball.

00:15:36.204 --> 00:15:38.424
Unknown: I mean, there are maybe...

00:15:38.424 --> 00:15:38.464
Wellby: [laughs]

00:15:39.824 --> 00:15:43.324
Wellby: But there's not, there's no, like, chemical weed killer or-

00:15:43.324 --> 00:15:45.044
DM: There is no chemical weed killer.

00:15:46.424 --> 00:15:49.624
DM: There are definitely magical weed killers, I would imagine, but.

00:15:50.764 --> 00:15:55.724
Wellby: But maybe some, like, I'm thinking of, like, some oil and, like, a pump or a sprayer or something.

00:15:55.724 --> 00:15:56.204
Wellby: I mean, if we-

00:15:57.824 --> 00:15:59.824
Seeker: What could possibly go wrong?

00:15:59.824 --> 00:16:00.204
Wellby: Yeah.

00:16:02.564 --> 00:16:08.464
Wellby: I mean, it's sort of a terrible idea, but, and we probably don't want to announce our presence, but to burn our way through something.

00:16:09.704 --> 00:16:20.284
Seeker: Does dwarven architecture ever encounter, like, really, like, you come back, you know, just a second later, like 150 years later after you're building your thing, and you come back and you're like, "Oh, no,"

00:16:20.284 --> 00:16:22.704
Seeker: you know, covered with a small forest.

00:16:22.704 --> 00:16:25.684
Seeker: Like, what do, what do dwarves do in that situation?

00:16:25.684 --> 00:16:31.204
DM: Um, usually chop down the forest and use it because wood is precious.

00:16:31.204 --> 00:16:32.004
Seeker: Hmm.

00:16:32.004 --> 00:16:32.204
Kenzo: Hmm.

00:16:35.464 --> 00:16:38.344
Kenzo: Um, how many, how, how many days ride is it to, uh, Raven's Hold?

00:16:39.924 --> 00:16:45.024
DM: Depends on how h- hard you wanna push yourself and, uh, you know,

00:16:46.704 --> 00:16:47.014
DM: how

00:16:48.684 --> 00:16:58.684
DM: fast you wanna travel. So, um, you know, if you're just, like, riding flat out without, you know, multiple horses or, like, pushing your horses past

00:16:58.684 --> 00:17:01.224
DM: the limits of their endurance or anything like that, um,

00:17:02.664 --> 00:17:03.944
DM: you know, it's probably about

00:17:05.884 --> 00:17:08.484
DM: four and a half to five days.

00:17:08.484 --> 00:17:09.464
Kenzo: Hmm.

00:17:09.464 --> 00:17:11.664
DM: You could... Four and a half days if you, if you...

00:17:11.664 --> 00:17:12.834
DM: So there's a, there's a,

00:17:14.104 --> 00:17:20.184
DM: not really a road, but there's, like, a wagon route, a trade route that heads sort of north and sort of meanders.

00:17:20.184 --> 00:17:20.644
DM: That's the,

00:17:21.744 --> 00:17:31.604
DM: the dotted line. And if you sort of, you can, this is not totally accurate, but you can shave a little bit of time off by cutting across the wilderness, probably about 20 miles, like,

00:17:32.804 --> 00:17:36.344
DM: mo- you know, three quarters of a day off or something like that.

00:17:36.344 --> 00:17:37.414
Seeker: Hmm.

00:17:37.414 --> 00:17:37.504
Kenzo: Hmm.

00:17:37.504 --> 00:17:37.684
DM: Um,

00:17:39.944 --> 00:17:43.784
DM: you know, if you're riding hard at a fast pace, um, you know,

00:17:45.024 --> 00:17:51.424
DM: it's gonna be hard to, you know, pay much attention to what's around you and, um, uh-

00:17:53.104 --> 00:17:53.704
Kenzo: Right. Right

00:17:53.704 --> 00:17:56.924
DM: ... you know, you won't really have time to, you know, try and

00:17:58.224 --> 00:17:59.904
DM: find water and stuff like that.

00:17:59.904 --> 00:18:03.363
DM: I mean, you can probably carry enough water to at least get you there.

00:18:03.364 --> 00:18:03.564
DM: Um,

00:18:05.424 --> 00:18:06.864
DM: but water's heavy.

00:18:06.864 --> 00:18:09.844
Kenzo: But we're, but we're, we're also trying to, like, observe stuff as we go.

00:18:09.844 --> 00:18:09.994
DM: Right.

00:18:09.994 --> 00:18:12.644
Kenzo: I mean, yeah, we're trying to get there, but we're also just trying to...

00:18:12.644 --> 00:18:15.894
Kenzo: So it's not like, um, we're trying to, like, go as fast as we can-

00:18:15.894 --> 00:18:15.894
DM: Right

00:18:15.894 --> 00:18:16.424
Kenzo: ... no matter what.

00:18:16.424 --> 00:18:16.483
DM: Right.

00:18:16.484 --> 00:18:21.382
Kenzo: I think we probably wanna go at a pace where we can still Take in the...

00:18:21.382 --> 00:18:31.431
Kenzo: Uh, 'cause the, the other kind of related strategic question is, um, we're, we're trying to meet back at the temple at some point to meet with Alesh, 'cause ideally she's searched a bunch of shit, we search a bunch of

00:18:31.432 --> 00:18:33.652
Kenzo: stuff, and then we're gonna converge.

00:18:33.652 --> 00:18:33.712
DM: Right, right.

00:18:33.712 --> 00:18:35.312
Kenzo: Did we decide on, um, with her

00:18:36.832 --> 00:18:39.992
Kenzo: how long we were gonna take? Like, were we gonna take two weeks to explore-

00:18:39.992 --> 00:18:41.932
DM: So, so she is...

00:18:41.932 --> 00:18:52.992
DM: She left the council to, uh, confer with Kisa about who should be sent in her place to scout, and also to,

00:18:52.992 --> 00:19:01.912
DM: uh... 'Cause she was gonna travel not by herself, but with a couple of Havdar's companions, other people who were skilled

00:19:03.332 --> 00:19:05.272
DM: riders and, um,

00:19:06.852 --> 00:19:13.702
DM: relatively stealthy, sneaky types on horseback, uh, so that they could run away from...

00:19:13.702 --> 00:19:16.132
DM: hide or run away from danger if they encountered it.

00:19:16.132 --> 00:19:18.072
DM: They're not really fighting types.

00:19:18.072 --> 00:19:18.252
DM: Um,

00:19:20.092 --> 00:19:26.132
DM: so she's probably doing that, but, uh, you could certainly talk to her at some point this afternoon and kind of

00:19:27.592 --> 00:19:29.552
DM: figure all that stuff out if you want to.

00:19:29.552 --> 00:19:31.372
DM: I think whatever you propose

00:19:33.012 --> 00:19:34.712
DM: she would be fine with.

00:19:37.252 --> 00:19:38.852
Wellby: So Tim, I-

00:19:38.852 --> 00:19:38.972
DM: Oh, I-

00:19:38.972 --> 00:19:39.972
Wellby: Go ahead.

00:19:39.972 --> 00:19:46.332
DM: So I, I was just gonna say, at a, at a sort of normal pace it would be six to seven days travel.

00:19:46.332 --> 00:19:46.392
DM: Um,

00:19:49.452 --> 00:19:53.372
DM: so six days if you cut overland, and seven days on the road.

00:19:53.372 --> 00:20:03.592
DM: And if you wanna... If you're going, like, slow and stealthy, trying to hide, um, and trying to, you know, really pay careful attention to everything around, you're talking, like, nine to 10

00:20:03.592 --> 00:20:05.252
DM: days to get there.

00:20:05.252 --> 00:20:15.512
DM: That's, I mean, it was... So the halflings took about 10 days, um, on the road to get here from there, but of course traveling at a relatively slow pace

00:20:15.512 --> 00:20:17.172
DM: with wagons and things like that.

00:20:20.272 --> 00:20:24.432
Wellby: So W- Wellby's gonna be, uh, gonna do two things.

00:20:24.432 --> 00:20:29.831
Wellby: Uh, the first is he's gonna unpack everything he owns and repack it back together again.

00:20:29.831 --> 00:20:31.032
Wellby: Like, just go through...

00:20:31.032 --> 00:20:33.792
Wellby: He is a, a thorough planner.

00:20:33.792 --> 00:20:38.892
Wellby: He's gonna sit out front and go through every arrowhead, resharpening it.

00:20:38.892 --> 00:20:46.272
Wellby: Um, but then the other thing I wanna do is go back to the halflings again and see if I can get more information out of-

00:20:46.272 --> 00:20:46.592
DM: Mm.

00:20:47.812 --> 00:20:48.112
DM: Okay

00:20:48.112 --> 00:20:48.392
Wellby: ... that gang. And then

00:20:49.872 --> 00:20:53.312
Wellby: we not have to role play through the encounter, uh,

00:20:54.552 --> 00:20:56.832
Wellby: you know, in the interest of time. But that's...

00:20:56.832 --> 00:21:04.252
Wellby: He's gonna go back again, and especially finding more information about, like, the details of how did he get through the wall, or where did he get through the wall, if that's possible.

00:21:04.252 --> 00:21:05.012
DM: Okay.

00:21:05.012 --> 00:21:07.592
Wellby: Just anything relevant at all.

00:21:07.592 --> 00:21:09.092
DM: Uh-huh. Okay. Uh,

00:21:10.572 --> 00:21:12.092
DM: right. S- okay.

00:21:12.092 --> 00:21:13.552
DM: So Wellby doing that. Um,

00:21:14.692 --> 00:21:16.032
DM: Delwath's shopping.

00:21:18.572 --> 00:21:19.572
DM: Anything else?

00:21:20.652 --> 00:21:20.982
Kenzo: I'm talking-

00:21:20.982 --> 00:21:24.912
DM: And then late afternoon you'll, you'll have a conversation with Alesh.

00:21:24.912 --> 00:21:26.792
Seeker: Um, Ikram about the book.

00:21:26.792 --> 00:21:28.852
DM: And talking to Ikram about the book, right.

00:21:29.872 --> 00:21:30.132
DM: Um,

00:21:33.452 --> 00:21:36.132
DM: okay. So, so why don't-

00:21:36.132 --> 00:21:36.172
Kenzo: So-

00:21:36.172 --> 00:21:37.192
DM: Yeah, go ahead.

00:21:37.192 --> 00:21:38.872
Kenzo: I was gonna say, um, Kenzo is gonna, um...

00:21:40.032 --> 00:21:42.032
Kenzo: If Candrosa's around, um-

00:21:42.032 --> 00:21:42.272
DM: Yep.

00:21:44.672 --> 00:21:55.052
Kenzo: Kenzo wants to spend a little bit of time, um, talking about, uh, kind of the Lakan, um, occult, and then also understanding

00:21:55.052 --> 00:21:59.572
Kenzo: more, a little bit more about the history of what's going on with, um, the Shakun.

00:21:59.572 --> 00:22:08.392
Kenzo: And, and also trying to learn if there's any, um, um, kind of mystic or occult related things that we might expect in the north.

00:22:08.392 --> 00:22:11.252
Kenzo: Like, is there, you know, are there other, um...

00:22:11.252 --> 00:22:14.842
Kenzo: Is Raven's Hold connected to any of these deities in any weird way?

00:22:14.842 --> 00:22:18.042
Kenzo: Are there, um... You know, just kind of get- getting more of the-

00:22:18.042 --> 00:22:18.042
DM: Right

00:22:18.042 --> 00:22:21.052
Kenzo: ... the, uh, the lay of the land there.

00:22:21.052 --> 00:22:21.932
DM: Okay. Um,

00:22:24.352 --> 00:22:26.922
DM: okay. So, uh, Wellby,

00:22:27.992 --> 00:22:33.992
DM: you head off to the halflings' encampment sort of outside the market, uh,

00:22:35.192 --> 00:22:36.352
DM: and as you,

00:22:37.372 --> 00:22:38.632
DM: uh...

00:22:38.632 --> 00:22:39.041
DM: Hold on.

00:22:41.792 --> 00:22:44.092
DM: So, so as you approach, um,

00:22:45.952 --> 00:22:56.772
DM: Callie comes out, sort of senses you coming, and com- comes and sort of cuts you off a little bit and kind of pulls you off to the side, uh, to talk and says,

00:22:56.772 --> 00:22:56.892
DM: uh,

00:22:59.412 --> 00:23:01.312
DM: "Wellby,

00:23:03.092 --> 00:23:06.412
DM: g- good to see you. It's, it's been a tough few days.

00:23:06.412 --> 00:23:06.532
DM: Uh,

00:23:09.412 --> 00:23:10.752
DM: my brother, he's...

00:23:10.752 --> 00:23:15.592
DM: Well, he's not getting worse so much, but he's definitely not getting better.

00:23:15.592 --> 00:23:21.572
DM: And a couple days ago at the market he had one of his episodes, and...

00:23:23.872 --> 00:23:26.032
DM: Well, m- my grandmother's very upset.

00:23:26.032 --> 00:23:28.932
DM: She, you know, we've sold hardly anything.

00:23:28.932 --> 00:23:29.472
DM: It's been a...

00:23:31.432 --> 00:23:34.492
DM: It's been very bad. If there's, if you have any good news at all,

00:23:36.272 --> 00:23:37.312
DM: I would love to hear it."

00:23:39.592 --> 00:23:40.172
Wellby: Well, uh,

00:23:42.312 --> 00:23:43.032
Wellby: uh, no,

00:23:44.712 --> 00:23:45.132
Wellby: I don't.

00:23:46.172 --> 00:23:46.592
Wellby: Um,

00:23:48.192 --> 00:23:48.512
Wellby: uh,

00:23:50.792 --> 00:23:54.832
Wellby: in a few days after the festival the town's gonna be moving west.

00:23:54.832 --> 00:23:56.652
Wellby: Most people are evacuating for safety.

00:23:59.332 --> 00:24:01.112
Wellby: Perhaps you'll have better luck there.

00:24:03.152 --> 00:24:03.832
DM: "We'd heard

00:24:05.292 --> 00:24:07.612
DM: some rumors. I, I didn't know what to think."

00:24:09.812 --> 00:24:11.032
Wellby: [sighs] Yeah.

00:24:11.032 --> 00:24:12.252
Wellby: They'll move on to Tokra.

00:24:16.392 --> 00:24:17.652
DM: "Okay."

00:24:19.032 --> 00:24:24.452
Wellby: Is there anything else that you can tell me about Raven's Hold or the road between here and there?

00:24:24.452 --> 00:24:26.152
Wellby: I'm going with my companions to investigate.

00:24:29.836 --> 00:24:30.296
DM: Uh,

00:24:31.316 --> 00:24:32.336
DM: the road, well,

00:24:34.136 --> 00:24:35.656
DM: it's not a bad journey.

00:24:35.656 --> 00:24:38.456
DM: Um, the road, the track is clear the entire way.

00:24:38.456 --> 00:24:43.496
DM: Uh, there are a few, uh, good oases, good spots to get water.

00:24:43.496 --> 00:24:43.716
DM: Um,

00:24:45.936 --> 00:24:46.996
DM: you know, it's...

00:24:48.776 --> 00:24:52.656
DM: In the past, it's always felt like the safest part of the journey.

00:24:52.656 --> 00:25:03.536
DM: Like, you come through the mountains, and it's always a little dicey, but the, you know, last 10 days or so through Dunmar, uh, are relaxing and, you know.

00:25:03.536 --> 00:25:07.936
DM: The, the plains up north are, are much more hospitable than the deserts to the south.

00:25:07.936 --> 00:25:10.156
DM: It's not quite so dry.

00:25:10.156 --> 00:25:10.396
DM: Um,

00:25:12.076 --> 00:25:13.736
DM: can be quite pretty at times even.

00:25:15.516 --> 00:25:17.756
DM: But now, I don't know, after what happened at Raven's Hold.

00:25:17.756 --> 00:25:21.656
DM: Nothing chased us particularly.

00:25:21.656 --> 00:25:23.536
DM: We didn't have any trouble on the road, but,

00:25:26.876 --> 00:25:29.016
DM: you know, I've been hearing all sorts of rumors.

00:25:29.016 --> 00:25:33.616
DM: I don't know if the same will hold in the future.

00:25:33.616 --> 00:25:38.756
DM: Um, Raven's Hold itself is set a, a few miles off the track,

00:25:40.276 --> 00:25:41.036
DM: uh, east.

00:25:43.296 --> 00:25:44.716
DM: But it's pretty obvious.

00:25:44.716 --> 00:25:46.376
DM: I mean, you can see it from the road.

00:25:46.376 --> 00:25:56.376
DM: The, the mountains themselves form a sharp rise f- from the plains, and Raven's Hold is sat in a,

00:25:56.376 --> 00:25:57.976
DM: in a valley. Uh, there's a,

00:26:00.016 --> 00:26:03.146
DM: a wall across the valley, and you can see the, um,

00:26:04.216 --> 00:26:11.476
DM: the outer wall and the, the keep, the fortress set up a little bit in the valley from the road.

00:26:14.196 --> 00:26:14.416
DM: Um,

00:26:15.996 --> 00:26:19.876
DM: it's never been a place that seemed very hospitable to explore.

00:26:19.876 --> 00:26:24.295
DM: I think there's some dwarven caves there or something like that as well, uh, I've heard.

00:26:25.596 --> 00:26:25.796
DM: Um,

00:26:28.696 --> 00:26:29.276
DM: there,

00:26:30.576 --> 00:26:33.256
DM: there is a, a small str- a river.

00:26:33.256 --> 00:26:40.825
DM: Well, I shouldn't say a river, a stream that flows out, and it's, the gatehouse seems to, like it's fallen in.

00:26:40.825 --> 00:26:47.096
DM: So it's, if you wade through the river, it's pretty easy to just get in that way.

00:26:47.096 --> 00:26:49.956
DM: But I've never explored myself.

00:26:49.956 --> 00:26:52.136
DM: It's always seemed a little bit too

00:26:53.536 --> 00:26:55.096
DM: creepy for my taste.

00:26:55.096 --> 00:26:57.576
Wellby: Is there any approach to it that isn't visible?

00:26:59.456 --> 00:27:04.596
Wellby: Uh, I mean, presumably not, since it's built as a fortress, but there's no...

00:27:06.296 --> 00:27:07.596
DM: None that I'm aware of.

00:27:10.216 --> 00:27:10.416
Wellby: Hmm.

00:27:11.496 --> 00:27:12.936
Wellby: Hmm.

00:27:12.936 --> 00:27:15.136
Wellby: Do you know where the dwarven caves are?

00:27:16.616 --> 00:27:17.156
Wellby: Worth finding out.

00:27:17.156 --> 00:27:17.236
DM: No.

00:27:18.916 --> 00:27:20.476
DM: I've just heard rumors.

00:27:22.876 --> 00:27:25.456
DM: If you're going there, and she, like, sort of looks,

00:27:27.396 --> 00:27:30.316
DM: you know, very much concerned.

00:27:30.316 --> 00:27:33.036
DM: If you find anything that might help Ander, please,

00:27:34.796 --> 00:27:38.896
DM: I guess we're gonna be heading to Tokra with everybody else, but please

00:27:40.536 --> 00:27:42.165
DM: t- you know, come, tell me,

00:27:43.936 --> 00:27:45.276
DM: let us know, because

00:27:46.916 --> 00:27:49.776
DM: I don't know how much longer he's gonna be able to live like this.

00:27:53.356 --> 00:27:54.636
DM: We'd really appreciate it.

00:27:56.356 --> 00:27:58.656
Wellby: We'll see you in Tokra, and if not Tokra,

00:28:00.196 --> 00:28:00.476
Wellby: then

00:28:02.136 --> 00:28:03.936
Wellby: beyond, Sangara.

00:28:06.456 --> 00:28:07.416
DM: We'll see you in Tokra.

00:28:12.016 --> 00:28:15.276
DM: Is there anything else you'd like to try and learn from the halflings?

00:28:15.276 --> 00:28:21.456
Wellby: Uh, I don't think so. I mean, u- unless we can get anything more out of her brother that's not just rambling

00:28:22.576 --> 00:28:23.676
Wellby: more of the same.

00:28:23.736 --> 00:28:27.896
DM: I mean, you can try reaching out with your telepathy.

00:28:27.896 --> 00:28:28.066
DM: Um-

00:28:28.066 --> 00:28:28.165
Wellby: Mm-hmm.

00:28:29.636 --> 00:28:30.036
DM: Make a,

00:28:32.876 --> 00:28:35.516
DM: make just a straight wisdom check.

00:28:35.516 --> 00:28:39.016
Wellby: Okay.

00:28:41.776 --> 00:28:52.396
DM: So after you talk to her, uh, you kind of reach out to Ander a little bit and just try and see, and his, I mean, his

00:28:52.396 --> 00:28:54.116
DM: thoughts are

00:28:55.176 --> 00:29:01.116
DM: very much consumed by this, um, you know, this repeated mantra of, "The master is coming.

00:29:01.116 --> 00:29:02.616
DM: The master will consume the world."

00:29:03.656 --> 00:29:05.576
DM: Um, just what you've heard before.

00:29:05.576 --> 00:29:16.196
DM: Um, but sort of trying to concentrate a little bit on, on what he's saying a- and the way the chant is going, you're, you're able to kind of, uh,

00:29:16.196 --> 00:29:24.146
DM: you know, like you did once before, sort of try and probe, you know, give him probing questions to, to, um, to try and get images.

00:29:24.146 --> 00:29:25.736
DM: A- and you do see

00:29:26.796 --> 00:29:37.036
DM: the detail of him, like, walking, splashing in this river, which didn't seem too d- too deep, and then looking past the wall.

00:29:37.036 --> 00:29:44.436
DM: There's a waterfall coming down from the mountains, fall- water falling into a pool that then flows into the river.

00:29:44.436 --> 00:29:46.636
DM: To your right, up ahead, there's a,

00:29:47.736 --> 00:29:58.196
DM: a second wall that's mostly crumbling, and then a intact tower that's set against a cliff, and then there's a sort of field stretching on either side of the river

00:29:58.196 --> 00:29:58.936
DM: of, um,

00:30:00.056 --> 00:30:10.096
DM: you know, sort of scrabbl- some trees and, and better vegetation right along the river, and then, uh, sort of as the, as it slopes up away from the

00:30:10.096 --> 00:30:13.585
DM: river, on the slopes, the sort of scraggly plants.

00:30:13.585 --> 00:30:23.916
DM: And you see yourself as Ander kind of walking towards the keep through this field of these scraggly vines, and then it's just this flash

00:30:23.916 --> 00:30:24.296
DM: of

00:30:26.216 --> 00:30:27.286
DM: hunger and

00:30:28.996 --> 00:30:39.168
DM: pain and this just raw desire for, um- To just consume whatever you can get, and you get kind

00:30:39.168 --> 00:30:40.358
DM: of kicked out of his mind.

00:30:45.048 --> 00:30:46.498
DM: And you hear this sort of faint

00:30:47.568 --> 00:30:51.438
DM: whimpering in the background, and then a scream, and then

00:30:52.668 --> 00:30:53.307
DM: silence.

00:30:59.428 --> 00:31:00.708
Wellby: All right. I think I'm good with that.

00:31:04.968 --> 00:31:06.008
DM: Okay. Um,

00:31:08.228 --> 00:31:12.468
DM: okay, so Seeker, you head back to the tavern, to Ikram's.

00:31:15.188 --> 00:31:19.348
DM: So as you approach, um, Ikram is,

00:31:20.888 --> 00:31:22.128
DM: uh, he seems...

00:31:22.128 --> 00:31:23.888
DM: There's a lot of, of people here.

00:31:23.888 --> 00:31:33.208
DM: There's, um, m- many new people, even people who weren't here for the festival seem to have been coming into town over the past couple of hours.

00:31:33.208 --> 00:31:33.448
DM: And,

00:31:34.708 --> 00:31:35.388
DM: uh, as you,

00:31:36.748 --> 00:31:40.688
DM: as you walk in, um, Ikram, uh, runs up

00:31:41.748 --> 00:31:43.448
DM: to you and is sort of like, uh,

00:31:44.548 --> 00:31:46.568
DM: "Have you heard? Have you seen all these people?

00:31:46.568 --> 00:31:49.388
DM: There's refugees starting to come from the south.

00:31:50.688 --> 00:31:52.508
DM: Where, where are your companions?

00:31:52.508 --> 00:31:53.008
DM: Do you know what's,

00:31:54.208 --> 00:31:55.408
DM: you know what's happening? This is...

00:31:56.828 --> 00:31:59.208
DM: These are bad times for C- for Karawa."

00:32:03.348 --> 00:32:04.368
DM: You're muted.

00:32:04.368 --> 00:32:08.318
Seeker: I'll gather them up. They've, they've, uh, they've gone off on various errands,

00:32:09.968 --> 00:32:11.278
Seeker: but that's good to know.

00:32:11.278 --> 00:32:11.988
Seeker: Where are they coming from?

00:32:13.568 --> 00:32:17.408
DM: From the south, from, from Bas Udda mostly.

00:32:17.408 --> 00:32:19.828
DM: It seems like the town was, the village was attacked.

00:32:21.148 --> 00:32:21.778
Seeker: Mm-hmm.

00:32:21.778 --> 00:32:25.348
DM: Uh, the, the few who survived are, are now coming to us, and

00:32:28.228 --> 00:32:29.668
DM: I, I don't know. I don't know if the,

00:32:30.768 --> 00:32:34.698
DM: whatever attacked them is on their heels or...

00:32:35.768 --> 00:32:45.887
DM: We just don't know. J- they say there's, there's many following behind them, and Havdar's troops are, are trying to protect the rear

00:32:45.887 --> 00:32:51.728
DM: and, and slow down whatever it is, but it sounds bad.

00:32:51.728 --> 00:32:52.488
Seeker: Hmm.

00:32:54.488 --> 00:32:55.528
Seeker: We'll have to try to learn more-

00:32:55.528 --> 00:32:55.748
DM: Are you-

00:32:55.748 --> 00:32:57.508
Seeker: ... about whatever this bad thing is.

00:32:57.508 --> 00:32:58.748
DM: Are you, uh...

00:32:58.748 --> 00:33:00.258
DM: I've heard Havdar's recruiting.

00:33:00.258 --> 00:33:03.708
DM: Are you gonna, are you joining his band?

00:33:04.788 --> 00:33:08.308
Seeker: Our party had planned to go north to Raven's Hold.

00:33:10.568 --> 00:33:11.768
DM: Ah, interesting.

00:33:11.768 --> 00:33:12.988
DM: Interesting. Um,

00:33:14.868 --> 00:33:14.888
DM: I,

00:33:16.218 --> 00:33:18.068
DM: I, I don't know what's up there.

00:33:18.068 --> 00:33:20.008
DM: That sounds dangerous.

00:33:22.028 --> 00:33:25.448
Seeker: We've heard that there are some, some bad magics up there.

00:33:30.648 --> 00:33:30.788
Seeker: Um-

00:33:30.788 --> 00:33:32.788
Kenzo: You are talking to the, uh, gossipy town,

00:33:33.908 --> 00:33:35.718
DM: town guy. [laughs]

00:33:35.718 --> 00:33:36.588
Seeker: [laughs] It's true, but, uh,

00:33:37.668 --> 00:33:40.588
Seeker: I don't have any reason to think that our plan is secret.

00:33:42.668 --> 00:33:44.848
DM: No, I don't think it's particularly secret.

00:33:44.848 --> 00:33:45.068
DM: Um...

00:33:48.788 --> 00:33:49.538
Seeker: He does seem to know people.

00:33:49.538 --> 00:33:54.868
DM: You might not wanna spread around that there's, like, evil, bad magic coming for Karawa or something like that.

00:33:54.868 --> 00:33:56.688
DM: That might make people nervous, but...

00:33:56.688 --> 00:33:59.368
Seeker: I guess so. It's kind of coming from all directions, though.

00:33:59.368 --> 00:34:00.928
Seeker: Everyone is already terrified-

00:34:00.928 --> 00:34:00.938
DM: Yeah

00:34:00.938 --> 00:34:01.078
Seeker: ... of whatever this thing-

00:34:01.078 --> 00:34:01.568
DM: Right. That's true

00:34:01.568 --> 00:34:07.688
Seeker: ... like. Also, I'm not totally clear on, on why there's been no discussion of, like, canceling the religious festival.

00:34:07.688 --> 00:34:08.378
Seeker: Is it that important?

00:34:09.408 --> 00:34:10.758
Seeker: Because, like...

00:34:10.758 --> 00:34:12.008
Seeker: Anyway, um,

00:34:14.268 --> 00:34:14.348
Seeker: I mean-

00:34:14.348 --> 00:34:16.528
DM: Yeah. So I mean, it's like, uh...

00:34:18.868 --> 00:34:23.688
DM: Uh, yeah, nobody, everybody is even more desperate for the religious festival because of-

00:34:23.688 --> 00:34:24.508
Seeker: Right

00:34:24.508 --> 00:34:32.148
DM: ... they want the g- the actual real gods who can actually protect them to come actually protect them.

00:34:32.148 --> 00:34:33.648
Seeker: That's the mindset.

00:34:33.648 --> 00:34:33.808
Seeker: Um-

00:34:33.808 --> 00:34:37.808
DM: You know, they're putting all their eggs in the Jeevali basket.

00:34:39.368 --> 00:34:41.948
Seeker: Ikram, I, I have a, I have a question.

00:34:41.948 --> 00:34:50.987
Seeker: I know it's, uh, might seem less important at a time like this, but I'm, I'm wondering if you know anything about the origin of the, the book of runes that, that I received.

00:34:53.808 --> 00:34:57.008
DM: Oh, interesting. I thought it was a, a blank book.

00:34:58.048 --> 00:34:58.728
DM: It's a...

00:34:58.788 --> 00:34:59.128
Seeker: Oh, yeah.

00:35:00.688 --> 00:35:01.988
DM: It's a book of runes?

00:35:03.388 --> 00:35:06.028
Seeker: There... Tim, there were runes on the cover, right?

00:35:06.028 --> 00:35:07.968
DM: There were runes on the cover, yeah.

00:35:07.968 --> 00:35:08.428
Seeker: Um,

00:35:09.608 --> 00:35:13.078
Seeker: it turned out to have some, some, [clears throat] some special properties, that's-

00:35:15.168 --> 00:35:15.288
DM: [coughs]

00:35:15.288 --> 00:35:15.528
Seeker: Uh-

00:35:15.528 --> 00:35:16.928
DM: Interesting. Interesting.

00:35:16.928 --> 00:35:17.078
DM: Uh,

00:35:18.908 --> 00:35:20.907
DM: I, I actually don't know who dropped that off.

00:35:20.908 --> 00:35:21.888
DM: Um, it...

00:35:23.748 --> 00:35:25.188
DM: I didn't see anybody.

00:35:26.428 --> 00:35:27.388
Seeker: Hmm.

00:35:27.388 --> 00:35:31.838
DM: It was just left here with a note, "See that this gets to the heroes of Karawa."

00:35:33.388 --> 00:35:35.538
DM: Um, maybe somebody-

00:35:35.538 --> 00:35:35.548
Seeker: Yeah

00:35:35.548 --> 00:35:36.138
DM: ... knew that

00:35:37.228 --> 00:35:38.188
DM: you were interested.

00:35:42.848 --> 00:35:43.928
Seeker: I wonder who would know.

00:35:45.708 --> 00:35:46.808
DM: I wonder, too. I mean,

00:35:48.168 --> 00:35:53.928
DM: certainly you've ma- made a bit of a name for yourself in the past weeks that you've been here.

00:35:53.928 --> 00:35:54.468
Seeker: Mm-hmm.

00:35:56.588 --> 00:35:58.908
DM: We do have a lot of people in town for the festival.

00:36:00.488 --> 00:36:04.667
DM: Haven't seen that many dwarves, but maybe it wasn't a dwarf.

00:36:04.668 --> 00:36:05.108
DM: I don't know.

00:36:06.388 --> 00:36:07.008
Seeker: Hmm.

00:36:08.728 --> 00:36:11.468
Seeker: You don't happen to have the note that was left with it, do you?

00:36:13.388 --> 00:36:15.268
DM: Yeah, here. He hands it to you.

00:36:15.268 --> 00:36:16.208
Seeker: Okay.

00:36:16.208 --> 00:36:18.008
DM: I mean, he had given it to you already.

00:36:18.008 --> 00:36:19.088
DM: You have, you have it.

00:36:19.088 --> 00:36:19.468
Seeker: Oh, okay.

00:36:21.008 --> 00:36:25.728
Seeker: And as we all know, handwriting analysis is an imp- important part of geologic study.

00:36:27.968 --> 00:36:28.358
Wellby: [laughs]

00:36:28.358 --> 00:36:28.358
Seeker: Um...

00:36:29.898 --> 00:36:32.388
Seeker: [coughs] Okay, I guess I'm not gonna find out much more.

00:36:32.388 --> 00:36:32.648
Seeker: Um,

00:36:34.008 --> 00:36:38.948
Seeker: the other thing it occurred to me to do is, is to go back over to the temple, which I guess is not far away.

00:36:38.948 --> 00:36:39.108
DM: Yep.

00:36:40.232 --> 00:36:46.692
Seeker: And I keep thinking about this oddity of the temple somehow being older than the mesa.

00:36:46.692 --> 00:36:47.332
Seeker: And so I'm just gonna

00:36:48.512 --> 00:36:50.852
Seeker: look around the edges, the seams, the

00:36:51.972 --> 00:36:53.792
Seeker: intersection points between

00:36:55.452 --> 00:36:57.491
Seeker: the, the worked stone and the raw.

00:36:57.492 --> 00:36:58.552
DM: Yeah. So,

00:37:00.112 --> 00:37:02.412
DM: um, uh, make, um,

00:37:04.332 --> 00:37:05.991
DM: make an investigation check.

00:37:11.312 --> 00:37:15.252
Kenzo: Eric, this is also useful as you try to figure out how best to fortify the temple.

00:37:15.252 --> 00:37:15.372
Seeker: Mm.

00:37:17.412 --> 00:37:17.832
Seeker: Good point.

00:37:18.892 --> 00:37:20.352
DM: So it's, um...

00:37:24.012 --> 00:37:32.472
DM: So, so the temple is, is built of y- largely, like, mud bricks and stuff that are, um, you know, kind of...

00:37:32.472 --> 00:37:36.692
DM: And, and it's sort of like what you have in your screen, your backdrop, right?

00:37:36.692 --> 00:37:39.212
DM: It's like bricks and then carved a little bit.

00:37:39.212 --> 00:37:39.412
DM: Um,

00:37:40.692 --> 00:37:41.072
DM: and

00:37:42.192 --> 00:37:42.502
DM: the...

00:37:43.612 --> 00:37:53.452
DM: So it seems like almost like there's, there's the stone that's, like, right adjacent to the temple itself doesn't look so different than the temple.

00:37:53.452 --> 00:38:00.751
DM: But, like, as you sort of follow the edge of the cliff, maybe 50, 100 feet away, you start to see...

00:38:00.752 --> 00:38:01.932
DM: It's almost like, um,

00:38:03.712 --> 00:38:05.812
DM: it's almost like tree rings.

00:38:05.812 --> 00:38:12.012
DM: Like, as you go out, the, the s- stone seems...

00:38:12.012 --> 00:38:22.212
DM: I mean, it's hard to say the stone seems newer, but it's like there's this very strange pattern of weathering, where the further you get away from the temple, the less weathered the stone seems to be,

00:38:22.212 --> 00:38:26.142
DM: and the more you see, like, these clean, sharp lines and, um,

00:38:27.612 --> 00:38:32.852
DM: you know, uh, like very, like even fine sandstone that's, like,

00:38:34.172 --> 00:38:37.572
DM: very sharp and doesn't seem to be smoothed out at all.

00:38:38.692 --> 00:38:44.332
DM: Like, almost perfect right angle corners, like little steps going up and things.

00:38:44.332 --> 00:38:45.672
DM: And then it seems to vary a bit.

00:38:45.672 --> 00:38:49.972
DM: Like, it's not consistently this way as you sort of move, um,

00:38:53.452 --> 00:38:59.712
DM: uh, as you move either to the left or the right from the main entrance.

00:38:59.712 --> 00:39:00.931
DM: It doesn't seem like it's...

00:39:00.932 --> 00:39:10.032
DM: There's, like, a gradient away from the entrance, and there's, like, these patches, almost a new, what seems almost like new stone, and then you'll get to a little weathered section, and there's a patch of new stone.

00:39:12.292 --> 00:39:13.372
Seeker: Can I, um-

00:39:13.372 --> 00:39:14.752
DM: It's... I've never seen anything like this.

00:39:16.872 --> 00:39:20.012
Seeker: Try to take some samples for study.

00:39:20.012 --> 00:39:20.352
DM: Mm-hmm.

00:39:20.412 --> 00:39:23.871
Seeker: Take them away. Uh, at a respectful distance from the actual temple facade.

00:39:23.872 --> 00:39:24.772
DM: Right, of course.

00:39:28.352 --> 00:39:28.832
Seeker: Um,

00:39:33.572 --> 00:39:36.552
Seeker: I'm trying to think of any other way that I might investigate that.

00:39:41.852 --> 00:39:43.952
Seeker: It's sort of all wrong, basically.

00:39:43.952 --> 00:39:46.072
Seeker: Like, time has flowed unevenly here, or

00:39:47.112 --> 00:39:49.212
Seeker: something new was inserted into something older,

00:39:50.472 --> 00:39:53.792
Seeker: or something old was inserted into something newer.

00:39:53.792 --> 00:39:55.632
DM: Yeah, it's, yeah.

00:39:55.632 --> 00:39:57.352
DM: It's hard to say exactly what.

00:39:57.352 --> 00:40:03.252
DM: It's almost, it's almost like there's patches of the, of the cliff face that are growing new stone,

00:40:04.732 --> 00:40:05.832
DM: if that's even a thing.

00:40:06.992 --> 00:40:08.612
Seeker: It's definitely not.

00:40:08.612 --> 00:40:09.871
DM: [laughs]

00:40:09.872 --> 00:40:10.992
Seeker: Wow, that's amazing.

00:40:10.992 --> 00:40:11.752
DM: It seems to be.

00:40:13.392 --> 00:40:14.892
Seeker: And does it...

00:40:14.892 --> 00:40:15.192
Seeker: Wow.

00:40:17.132 --> 00:40:20.532
Seeker: And I have no idea what timescale this would have happened over.

00:40:20.532 --> 00:40:22.572
DM: No, but you know the temple is supposed to be,

00:40:23.972 --> 00:40:25.572
DM: you know, predates the Great War.

00:40:28.252 --> 00:40:29.292
Seeker: So I have one

00:40:30.432 --> 00:40:33.672
Seeker: non-obvious conclusion from all of this that is very indirect.

00:40:34.972 --> 00:40:40.852
Seeker: I sit down next to this very strange wall at an intersection of the old and new areas, and I take from my pack

00:40:42.772 --> 00:40:45.132
Seeker: the newly bound, um,

00:40:46.152 --> 00:40:53.792
Seeker: uh, legendary staff of Kenso, full of illustrations of different poses over the course of a few seconds.

00:40:53.792 --> 00:40:59.452
Seeker: And I take it apart, unbinding it, and flip through it like a flip book.

00:40:59.452 --> 00:41:04.272
Seeker: You can see almost, it's almost like an animation of Kenso fighting, fighting, fighting.

00:41:04.272 --> 00:41:05.712
Seeker: [clears throat]

00:41:07.872 --> 00:41:11.272
Seeker: I take it all apart, lay them side by side in different arrangements,

00:41:12.432 --> 00:41:13.712
Seeker: try to think about time.

00:41:15.472 --> 00:41:16.152
Seeker: And then I take out

00:41:18.352 --> 00:41:19.032
Seeker: some maps,

00:41:21.252 --> 00:41:22.672
Seeker: and put them side by side,

00:41:24.012 --> 00:41:29.872
Seeker: and then start to make small copies of them, new ones, imagining landscapes changing.

00:41:29.872 --> 00:41:33.432
Seeker: Stack them up and run my finger through them like a f- a flip book.

00:41:33.432 --> 00:41:36.192
Seeker: And anyway, I'm imagining four-dimensional maps.

00:41:39.512 --> 00:41:41.052
DM: Nothing happens.

00:41:41.052 --> 00:41:43.752
Seeker: Oh, no, I'm not trying to cause any magic to happen.

00:41:43.752 --> 00:41:45.532
DM: Okay, okay. You're just sort of

00:41:46.732 --> 00:41:50.532
DM: interrogating your mind and trying to unders- wrap your arms around this.

00:41:50.532 --> 00:41:55.192
Kenzo: He's developing a mental framework through which to, uh, analyze this phenomenon that he's observed.

00:41:55.192 --> 00:41:56.292
DM: Exactly.

00:41:56.292 --> 00:41:59.632
Seeker: Well, because I've been making these very detailed maps, but they're static in time.

00:41:59.632 --> 00:41:59.672
