DM: 15 to,

01:05:32.176 --> 01:05:36.616
Delwath: 15 to, 16, 17 to 20-something, right?

01:05:36.616 --> 01:05:37.065
DM: You know.

01:05:37.065 --> 01:05:37.096
Kenzo: Right.

01:05:37.096 --> 01:05:39.536
Delwath: Expect us in three weeks and worry in four.

01:05:40.616 --> 01:05:41.096
Kenzo: So, but-

01:05:41.096 --> 01:05:41.366
DM: Okay

01:05:41.366 --> 01:05:43.676
Kenzo: ... we should think, we should think through this contingency, though.

01:05:43.676 --> 01:05:51.796
Kenzo: Um, you know, in the event that something happens to us, um, or something happens to you, and s- we, we need to...

01:05:51.796 --> 01:05:55.236
Kenzo: I, I think the most important thing we have to do is figure out how to send word.

01:05:55.236 --> 01:06:05.396
Kenzo: Um, there, you know, if, if something terrible happens, then, um, you know, hopefully o- at least one of us can return back to Karawa with, with tidings of, of what we found, and similarly

01:06:05.396 --> 01:06:10.696
Kenzo: for you and your, your, um, um, your comrades here heading south.

01:06:10.696 --> 01:06:10.976
Kenzo: Um,

01:06:13.016 --> 01:06:15.536
Kenzo: I don't know if there's any other, anything we can think of that would be clever.

01:06:17.476 --> 01:06:19.396
Wellby: Well, who... I wonder, um,

01:06:20.916 --> 01:06:28.816
Wellby: you know, we can leave, leave word here at the temple, but if you're gone, is there someone you trust in Tokra, or for that matter, in-

01:06:28.816 --> 01:06:28.906
Kenzo: Hmm

01:06:28.906 --> 01:06:37.376
Wellby: ... Askandi or Sangara, you know, farther flung places, that you would trust to leave word for us or for us to leave word for you?

01:06:37.376 --> 01:06:37.556
Kenzo: Yeah.

01:06:39.016 --> 01:06:40.836
DM: I mean, as much as he can be

01:06:42.496 --> 01:06:50.696
DM: difficult and arrogant sometimes, I trust Havdar has the best interests of the Dunmari at heart always.

01:06:52.376 --> 01:07:00.016
DM: I suspect that his plan is to see the people safely to Tokra and then return here to protect Candrosa and the temple.

01:07:00.016 --> 01:07:06.756
DM: So we could hopefully, uh, leave messages with him, and if the situation is bad enough,

01:07:08.336 --> 01:07:10.216
DM: he will be in Tokra,

01:07:11.296 --> 01:07:13.616
DM: hopefully with Candrosa if they had to flee.

01:07:17.776 --> 01:07:26.512
DM: And of course, Kisa will be in G- Tokra with the village, so- I, I would trust either of them as-

01:07:26.512 --> 01:07:26.521
Kenzo: Mm-hmm

01:07:26.521 --> 01:07:32.572
DM: ... messengers or as, as people to leave messages with, with my life.

01:07:34.832 --> 01:07:35.952
Kenzo: Okay. Hmm.

01:07:38.972 --> 01:07:39.212
Kenzo: Hmm.

01:07:41.392 --> 01:07:42.932
Kenzo: And how far south are you traveling?

01:07:42.932 --> 01:07:46.232
Kenzo: You're traveling south to the, uh, to the desert?

01:07:46.232 --> 01:07:47.572
Kenzo: To Askandi?

01:07:47.572 --> 01:07:49.812
DM: I... Not as, not to Askandi.

01:07:49.812 --> 01:07:59.912
DM: My hope is to travel past Bas Udda, as far south as the edge of the Garamjala Desert, east as far as seems safe,

01:07:59.912 --> 01:08:04.132
DM: north to the southern edge of the Great Gash, and then back west to Karawa.

01:08:05.872 --> 01:08:08.412
Kenzo: You know, one thing that may be useful, um,

01:08:09.472 --> 01:08:19.552
Kenzo: if you, when you pass through Bas Udda, if you can leave, leave word about what you've seen, and, um, um, you know, if there's somebody there that you know, in case we have to travel there and

01:08:19.552 --> 01:08:23.112
Kenzo: find, and find word about you, um, leave us the name of somebody you trust.

01:08:24.312 --> 01:08:29.091
DM: If the rumor, if the rumors are true, I don't think there's anybody alive left in Bas Udda.

01:08:29.091 --> 01:08:31.352
Kenzo: Ugh.

01:08:31.352 --> 01:08:32.072
Kenzo: Okay.

01:08:32.072 --> 01:08:38.032
DM: I, I think all these pe- Because she, like, gestures around to the sort of milling crowds that are gathering.

01:08:38.032 --> 01:08:39.612
DM: I think all these people-

01:08:39.612 --> 01:08:39.622
Kenzo: Yeah

01:08:39.622 --> 01:08:42.132
DM: ... are citizens of Bas Udda who escaped.

01:08:42.132 --> 01:08:44.052
Kenzo: The refugees, yeah.

01:08:44.052 --> 01:08:48.652
Kenzo: So the only signs of life between us and Tokra is right here in Karawa now.

01:08:50.132 --> 01:08:51.142
Kenzo: Gomat's been abandoned,

01:08:54.152 --> 01:08:54.412
Kenzo: right?

01:08:56.252 --> 01:08:56.492
DM: Yeah.

01:08:57.572 --> 01:08:57.962
DM: I mean,

01:08:59.312 --> 01:09:08.331
DM: of course, there's usually people traveling on the, the plains between here and Tokra, but as the village moves through, I don't know how many will stay.

01:09:08.332 --> 01:09:08.952
DM: I suspect

01:09:10.332 --> 01:09:16.392
DM: as word of what's happening here spreads, that most Dunmari will move east, will move west of the River Hara.

01:09:19.912 --> 01:09:21.352
Kenzo: Hmm.

01:09:21.352 --> 01:09:22.802
DM: Only some scouts and other,

01:09:25.232 --> 01:09:32.072
DM: you know, slightly, uh, [sneezes] slightly crazy people like us will be left.

01:09:35.772 --> 01:09:37.232
Kenzo: Ugh. Okay.

01:09:37.232 --> 01:09:43.531
Kenzo: Well, it sounds like then all we can do at this point is, uh, do our best to meet back here in three weeks or so.

01:09:44.552 --> 01:09:45.272
Kenzo: Um-

01:09:45.272 --> 01:09:45.552
DM: Yeah

01:09:45.552 --> 01:09:55.452
Kenzo: ... I guess one question for all of us is, um, after we meet back here, um, Candrosa and, uh, the mystics there will stay.

01:09:55.452 --> 01:09:57.612
Kenzo: Uh, we will learn what we learn.

01:09:57.612 --> 01:10:05.632
Kenzo: Havdar will return here with some forces, because there may be other unknown evil, uh, coming, headed this way.

01:10:06.772 --> 01:10:12.712
Kenzo: And then, um, and then we'll have to make some decisions, I suppose, at that point, given on what we know, based on what we know.

01:10:13.892 --> 01:10:14.172
Kenzo: Um,

01:10:18.272 --> 01:10:19.371
Kenzo: I suppose the only-

01:10:19.372 --> 01:10:19.442
DM: Yeah

01:10:19.442 --> 01:10:22.032
Kenzo: ... remaining question I had was...

01:10:22.032 --> 01:10:23.232
Kenzo: Oh, Seeker.

01:10:24.812 --> 01:10:26.452
Seeker: Just a, a, a detail.

01:10:26.452 --> 01:10:33.132
Seeker: I was curious if anybody knows anything already about what's at that, the tip of that southern edge of that

01:10:34.172 --> 01:10:34.652
Seeker: gorge.

01:10:36.512 --> 01:10:37.412
Seeker: You said you were gonna visit it.

01:10:38.612 --> 01:10:38.952
DM: Like, here?

01:10:40.872 --> 01:10:41.232
Seeker: Yes.

01:10:43.472 --> 01:10:43.892
DM: Uh,

01:10:45.892 --> 01:10:49.112
DM: no. I mean, people don't usually go

01:10:50.232 --> 01:10:53.372
DM: east of the, like, into the Blasted Plain, right?

01:10:53.372 --> 01:10:54.772
DM: So, um,

01:10:55.812 --> 01:10:57.592
DM: although things are changing, right?

01:10:57.592 --> 01:10:58.992
DM: But historically, there was,

01:11:00.332 --> 01:11:04.352
DM: like Candrosa's told you, right, there were these sort of enchantments that ran sort of,

01:11:05.512 --> 01:11:05.832
DM: like,

01:11:06.972 --> 01:11:08.692
DM: here. Well, let me see if I can...

01:11:10.552 --> 01:11:18.552
DM: Ran, like, you know, like that, and then like that, and then like that, you know, sort of in a zigzag line,

01:11:20.252 --> 01:11:22.332
DM: stopping on the northern edge of the desert.

01:11:22.332 --> 01:11:30.512
DM: But this desert is much harsher than what's around Karawa, so, so people don't think anybody travels through there.

01:11:30.512 --> 01:11:33.431
DM: And nobody really went east of that.

01:11:33.432 --> 01:11:34.672
DM: So a lot of this is unknown.

01:11:34.672 --> 01:11:36.952
DM: People are scouting out there, because it seems like this,

01:11:38.352 --> 01:11:42.892
DM: this border has become fluid in a way that it hasn't in the past.

01:11:42.892 --> 01:11:43.632
Seeker: Hmm. Okay.

01:11:47.952 --> 01:11:48.392
DM: Right, so

01:11:49.452 --> 01:11:52.072
DM: Alesh Had, had said before she had never rode past the,

01:11:53.612 --> 01:11:55.232
DM: you know, like, the canyon, right?

01:11:55.232 --> 01:11:56.112
DM: She'd never rode

01:11:57.432 --> 01:11:58.072
DM: that far.

01:11:59.092 --> 01:11:59.372
DM: Yes,

01:12:00.632 --> 01:12:01.892
DM: Kenzo.

01:12:01.892 --> 01:12:04.492
Kenzo: What can you tell us of, of Raven's Hold?

01:12:04.492 --> 01:12:08.002
Kenzo: Any, anything you can tell us about it, its history, what we might expect to find there.

01:12:08.002 --> 01:12:13.202
DM: Raven's Hold. Raven's Hold is quite interesting, because it was not just a fortress,

01:12:14.352 --> 01:12:18.152
DM: but it was a holdfast, a place where the few

01:12:19.232 --> 01:12:20.952
DM: people of the North...

01:12:20.952 --> 01:12:25.552
DM: So I, I don't know if you know this about our, our people's history, Kenzo, but,

01:12:26.672 --> 01:12:26.912
DM: uh,

01:12:28.192 --> 01:12:35.512
DM: you know, before the Great Upheavals and before the Great War, the, the North was still not so safe, right?

01:12:35.512 --> 01:12:37.662
DM: So there was forts and,

01:12:38.912 --> 01:12:43.452
DM: you know, wars with hobgoblins, wars with other stranger, more deadly creatures.

01:12:45.612 --> 01:12:47.972
DM: Uh, it's a place I'd love to visit sometimes.

01:12:47.972 --> 01:12:49.892
DM: I've heard... I, I've never been there.

01:12:49.892 --> 01:12:51.852
DM: I've heard from travelers, right?

01:12:51.852 --> 01:12:58.572
DM: So it's near the road. It's near one of the trade routes to Sombar, and so every now and then we'll get people, usually around this time, actually.

01:12:58.572 --> 01:12:59.731
DM: I wish that I hadn't been out

01:13:01.192 --> 01:13:08.252
DM: riding and scouting so much, because the festival is always one of the best times to hear stories and lore from travelers.

01:13:08.252 --> 01:13:10.291
DM: But sorry, I'm...

01:13:10.292 --> 01:13:12.672
DM: What was your question? I'm getting distracted.

01:13:12.672 --> 01:13:14.572
Kenzo: Uh, about Raven's Hold. What can you tell us about Raven's Hold?

01:13:14.572 --> 01:13:25.660
DM: Raven's Hold. So not only is it a defensive fortification, um, but it was also a place where, um-

01:13:25.660 --> 01:13:33.260
DM: At least what people say is that so there used to be dwarves that lived in those mountains as well.

01:13:33.260 --> 01:13:44.000
DM: I think many of them have moved southwest to the Yuvanti Mountains because, of course, with the upheavals, the southern part of the central mountain range has become

01:13:44.000 --> 01:13:46.390
DM: quite dangerous.

01:13:46.390 --> 01:13:49.420
DM: And that was and Raven's Hold was not just a place to

01:13:51.320 --> 01:13:54.520
DM: to defend Dunmari, but also a place to trade with dwarves.

01:13:54.520 --> 01:14:03.910
DM: And the story goes that there are ancient dwarven roads that run hundreds of miles underneath the mountains and that dwarves would

01:14:05.300 --> 01:14:15.320
DM: travel along these ancient roads south to this as one of the southernmost points of their kingdom and use that as a trading outpost

01:14:15.320 --> 01:14:15.700
DM: to,

01:14:18.680 --> 01:14:23.360
DM: you know, exchange grain for ore and things like that with the Dunmari.

01:14:23.360 --> 01:14:24.120
Seeker: Did I know about this?

01:14:26.520 --> 01:14:27.300
DM: Do what?

01:14:27.300 --> 01:14:28.040
Seeker: Did I know about that?

01:14:30.000 --> 01:14:33.080
DM: Well, this is quite far to the south of your homeland.

01:14:33.080 --> 01:14:36.440
DM: You know that there used to be other dwarven kingdoms.

01:14:37.680 --> 01:14:40.339
DM: So like you're part of the sort of more northern dwarves.

01:14:40.340 --> 01:14:44.570
DM: There definitely were like southern dwarves and they all left the mountains.

01:14:44.570 --> 01:14:47.820
DM: So the central mountain chain that runs up the middle of the continent

01:14:49.260 --> 01:14:52.040
DM: was at one point filled with dwarves.

01:14:52.040 --> 01:14:57.890
DM: But everything south of Ulrich Monastery basically fled after all the disaster.

01:14:57.890 --> 01:15:01.500
DM: And the people, your people who live north of there

01:15:03.640 --> 01:15:06.260
DM: sort of lost contact with the southern dwarves

01:15:07.580 --> 01:15:08.180
DM: after the war.

01:15:09.380 --> 01:15:13.240
DM: But you would have known there was a dwarven, there used to be a dwarven kingdom there.

01:15:13.240 --> 01:15:13.570
DM: And

01:15:14.960 --> 01:15:20.500
DM: you probably, I mean, given your history proficiency, you would have known that they traded with the Dunmari.

01:15:20.500 --> 01:15:26.220
DM: The details about Raven's Hold wouldn't have been part of your study, but.

01:15:26.220 --> 01:15:29.320
Seeker: But hundreds of miles of underground highways.

01:15:29.320 --> 01:15:32.660
DM: You've traveled on dwarven roads that go hundreds of miles underground.

01:15:32.660 --> 01:15:35.540
DM: This isn't that unusual to you.

01:15:35.540 --> 01:15:37.640
Seeker: But just that they're in this location, I didn't know.

01:15:37.640 --> 01:15:40.040
DM: That they're in this location, you didn't realize.

01:15:42.100 --> 01:15:42.940
Seeker: Word.

01:15:45.200 --> 01:15:48.100
DM: Alesh kind of looks at her companions and they think and she says,

01:15:50.180 --> 01:16:00.280
DM: you know that I could stay here talking history all evening, but friends, we must go if we're to make any distance tonight before it gets dark.

01:16:01.720 --> 01:16:03.209
DM: I wish you luck on your journey.

01:16:04.800 --> 01:16:06.380
DM: Stay safe.

01:16:06.380 --> 01:16:07.320
DM: Travel carefully.

01:16:08.400 --> 01:16:14.080
DM: And we'll meet back here hopefully in three weeks' time.

01:16:14.160 --> 01:16:15.420
Kenzo: You as well in safe travels.

01:16:16.460 --> 01:16:17.300
DM: And with that, she rides off.

01:16:22.380 --> 01:16:22.720
DM: Okay.

01:16:24.320 --> 01:16:26.420
DM: Anything else you want to do before

01:16:27.580 --> 01:16:28.220
DM: tomorrow,

01:16:29.510 --> 01:16:30.320
DM: the festival?

01:16:33.760 --> 01:16:35.360
Seeker: No. Well, nothing.

01:16:38.540 --> 01:16:42.320
Kenzo: Seeker, what do you think about fortifications for the temple?

01:16:42.320 --> 01:16:45.200
Kenzo: Have you begun thinking of the best plan there?

01:16:45.200 --> 01:16:45.500
Kenzo: And do you need help?

01:16:45.500 --> 01:16:46.180
DM: I'll bring it back, guys.

01:16:48.200 --> 01:16:52.880
Seeker: I've surveyed a little bit, but I'll need Tim's help to figure out what is actually possible.

01:16:52.880 --> 01:16:56.700
Seeker: So, for instance, my ability to shape the stone relies on it being a little bit loose.

01:16:57.900 --> 01:17:03.120
Seeker: And then my ability to, like, actually, like, help the non-magical fortification of it depends on

01:17:04.240 --> 01:17:05.800
Seeker: my masonry skills, I guess.

01:17:07.400 --> 01:17:10.720
DM: So I would say given your,

01:17:12.160 --> 01:17:13.100
DM: not just your,

01:17:14.709 --> 01:17:18.660
DM: the combination of your masonry skills and your,

01:17:20.840 --> 01:17:25.920
DM: you know, your magical ability to shape earth and stone, you don't have a lot of time.

01:17:25.920 --> 01:17:30.299
DM: You can't, like, build a whole another wall or something like that.

01:17:30.300 --> 01:17:36.510
DM: But I would say you'd be able to, you know, if you spend most of, you know, the afternoon, most of the next day,

01:17:37.620 --> 01:17:39.650
DM: sort of up until the festival on this,

01:17:41.180 --> 01:17:44.560
DM: you could make, you know, some pretty decent,

01:17:46.420 --> 01:17:53.640
DM: you know, something like a, you know, a secondary wall kind of in front of the temple.

01:17:53.640 --> 01:18:01.800
DM: And basically also just, like, spend a lot of time just, like, reinforcing doors and walls, basically, so that it would be much harder to break something down.

01:18:02.960 --> 01:18:07.000
DM: Temple's in pretty good shape because not very much of it sticks out of the mesa, right?

01:18:07.000 --> 01:18:09.800
DM: And so if you can just fortify the part that

01:18:11.920 --> 01:18:16.140
DM: sticks out of the cliff wall, like, nobody can get in through the cliff, right?

01:18:16.140 --> 01:18:16.880
DM: It's

01:18:16.880 --> 01:18:19.060
Seeker: There's no back entrance. There's no side entrance.

01:18:20.100 --> 01:18:24.240
DM: There's, well, none that you're aware of, none that Candrosa's told you about.

01:18:26.280 --> 01:18:27.260
DM: So I would say

01:18:28.460 --> 01:18:33.299
DM: if you want to just make a, let's see, what would this be?

01:18:37.120 --> 01:18:45.460
DM: I would say make an intelligence check and then add four, which is twice your proficiency bonus because using the,

01:18:46.780 --> 01:18:49.380
DM: using shape earth and stone

01:18:51.220 --> 01:18:54.700
DM: to aid you makes you much better at masonry.

01:18:54.700 --> 01:18:55.000
Seeker: Nice.

01:18:58.080 --> 01:19:01.940
Seeker: So wait, I just, I'm going to roll the intelligence check.

01:19:01.940 --> 01:19:04.760
DM: Roll an intelligence check and then add four to it.

01:19:04.760 --> 01:19:06.040
DM: So 18.

01:19:06.040 --> 01:19:06.400
Seeker: Nice.

01:19:10.160 --> 01:19:18.300
DM: Yeah. So you, it takes you, I'll say it takes you most of the next day and you'll come back to the inn just to sort of clean up for an hour before the festival starts.

01:19:18.300 --> 01:19:28.608
DM: But you feel pretty comfortable with the job that you've done and you think that You know, this, you've definitely made a difference,

01:19:28.608 --> 01:19:30.347
DM: right? It's gonna be much harder to...

01:19:30.348 --> 01:19:36.908
DM: Certainly, like, if somebody comes in and tries to s- to break the main door down or something like that, it's gonna be much more difficult.

01:19:36.908 --> 01:19:44.638
Seeker: One crucial thing is I've multiple times now used the trick of, of tripping up a monster by undercutting it.

01:19:44.638 --> 01:19:44.668
DM: Yeah.

01:19:44.668 --> 01:19:45.568
Seeker: So I'm gonna make like-

01:19:46.768 --> 01:19:48.228
DM: Some pit traps?

01:19:48.228 --> 01:19:50.028
Seeker: Just more like a moat, a dry moat.

01:19:50.028 --> 01:19:51.228
DM: Okay. Yeah, yeah.

01:19:51.228 --> 01:19:53.188
Seeker: With a, with a wall on the other side of it.

01:19:53.188 --> 01:19:53.888
DM: Yeah. Okay.

01:19:53.888 --> 01:19:53.908
Seeker: Um-

01:19:53.908 --> 01:19:55.748
DM: So that's perfect. Yeah, you can do that.

01:19:57.108 --> 01:20:03.768
DM: That's easy with mold earth and stone because the loose dirt around the base of the temple is quite easy for you to,

01:20:04.868 --> 01:20:05.108
DM: um,

01:20:06.768 --> 01:20:08.918
DM: it's quite easy for you to shape.

01:20:08.918 --> 01:20:13.268
Seeker: It also... There's also this, like, make normal terrain into difficult terrain.

01:20:13.268 --> 01:20:14.408
DM: Right.

01:20:14.408 --> 01:20:15.248
Seeker: The problem is-

01:20:15.248 --> 01:20:15.488
DM: Yeah

01:20:16.608 --> 01:20:18.948
Seeker: ... people will need to be able to get in and out a little bit.

01:20:18.948 --> 01:20:19.988
DM: Right.

01:20:19.988 --> 01:20:20.308
Seeker: Um-

01:20:21.328 --> 01:20:25.428
DM: Well, I mean, so you can do something like, you know, there's second story windows, right?

01:20:25.428 --> 01:20:29.468
DM: They can have a rope ladder and come in and out via-

01:20:29.468 --> 01:20:30.348
Seeker: Yeah

01:20:30.348 --> 01:20:36.368
DM: ... via something like that. So you could really fortify the, the front door such that it's really quite difficult to-

01:20:36.368 --> 01:20:36.718
Seeker: I had been-

01:20:36.718 --> 01:20:37.868
DM: ... get past

01:20:37.868 --> 01:20:43.888
Seeker: ... I had been thinking we had to do this after the festival, but it's a, the festival is with different gods, so not this temple.

01:20:43.888 --> 01:20:44.688
DM: Right.

01:20:44.688 --> 01:20:45.228
Seeker: Right, right.

01:20:45.228 --> 01:20:45.628
DM: Right.

01:20:45.628 --> 01:20:47.088
Seeker: [laughs] I was mixed up about that before.

01:20:47.088 --> 01:20:49.268
Seeker: Okay.

01:20:49.408 --> 01:20:49.768
DM: Um,

01:20:52.028 --> 01:20:52.388
DM: okay.

01:20:54.448 --> 01:20:58.128
DM: So, um, again, anything else?

01:20:58.128 --> 01:21:01.288
DM: So fortifications, you've sort of dealt with everything else you want to deal with.

01:21:02.428 --> 01:21:10.308
DM: Okay, so as you are, um, sort of get- preparing yourself for the festival, so the first night of the festival is, um,

01:21:11.788 --> 01:21:22.788
