01:32:03.232 --> 01:32:09.952
Riswynn: Well, because it is my family's ancestral home, my great-great-uncle was able to sketch me a bit of a map.

01:32:09.952 --> 01:32:15.532
Riswynn: So when I get there, I'm sure I'll be able to orient, um, given my knowledge of

01:32:16.752 --> 01:32:23.092
Riswynn: dwarvish hold layouts and familiarity with the type of architecture of my people.

01:32:23.092 --> 01:32:28.392
Riswynn: But, um, I haven't actually been there myself, yeah, since before my-

01:32:28.392 --> 01:32:32.492
Kenzo: I like gr- I l- I like grab Wellby, like as you're like kind of like affectionately grab Wellby.

01:32:33.672 --> 01:32:34.672
Kenzo: This guy loves maps.

01:32:35.732 --> 01:32:36.112
Kenzo: Loves them.

01:32:37.412 --> 01:32:40.792
Wellby: [laughs] On your maps, is there a way in underground?

01:32:40.792 --> 01:32:52.092
Wellby: Are there, are there ways inside that aren't, um, walking through the, um, you know, the, the field of, of insanity-making vines?

01:32:52.092 --> 01:32:56.272
Riswynn: Hmm. Well, insanity-making vines weren't on the map.

01:32:56.272 --> 01:33:00.532
Riswynn: Uh, [laughs] I may have to, uh,

01:33:01.552 --> 01:33:03.152
Riswynn: take a, a look from

01:33:04.912 --> 01:33:13.502
Riswynn: some perspective and sort of orient with the information that I have from years ago when, when Uncle Iferk lived there.

01:33:13.502 --> 01:33:13.832
Riswynn: Um...

01:33:16.312 --> 01:33:17.532
Wellby: Very good.

01:33:17.532 --> 01:33:20.832
Seeker: Well, it sounds like we'd all benefit from traveling together.

01:33:22.632 --> 01:33:24.212
Riswynn: I definitely don't want to brave

01:33:25.452 --> 01:33:30.932
Riswynn: whatever, uh, beasts have been plaguing the peoples of these areas, including maddening

01:33:32.372 --> 01:33:33.832
Riswynn: enchantments.

01:33:33.832 --> 01:33:35.072
Riswynn: Uh, that-

01:33:35.072 --> 01:33:41.172
Kenzo: Um, Riswynn, if you don't mind, uh, me being a bit, uh, blunt, but, um, can you fight?

01:33:41.172 --> 01:33:42.812
Kenzo: Can you...

01:33:42.812 --> 01:33:52.892
Kenzo: We're, this, we, we don't know what we're gonna find there at all, and, um, you know, I think we're, our, our, our party's only recently really learned over the past couple of weeks

01:33:52.892 --> 01:33:53.192
Kenzo: how to,

01:33:54.712 --> 01:33:59.432
Kenzo: how to defend, how to fight, how to, uh, kind of deal with unexpected danger.

01:34:01.312 --> 01:34:03.192
Kenzo: Um, I say this j- I say this, um,

01:34:04.672 --> 01:34:06.292
Kenzo: from a place of, uh, of, uh,

01:34:07.512 --> 01:34:17.492
Kenzo: you know, agre- agreement that, um, you know, traveling together would be wise, but also we have a very urgent quest, and, um, need to make sure that we're focused on that.

01:34:18.552 --> 01:34:21.132
Riswynn: Well, I can take care of myself and perhaps some of you, too.

01:34:21.132 --> 01:34:26.801
Riswynn: I mean, the war hammer comes as naturally to the dwarf as anything else, and with, uh,

01:34:28.732 --> 01:34:39.092
Riswynn: the amount of time that I've spent in devotion to the Baharzel, I've got some capabilities for spell casting that, um, help me out

01:34:39.092 --> 01:34:41.112
Riswynn: when I'm in a battle situation.

01:34:42.552 --> 01:34:42.572
Riswynn: So...

01:34:42.572 --> 01:34:44.522
Kenzo: What kind of spells, if you don't mind me asking?

01:34:46.812 --> 01:34:48.692
Riswynn: Divine ones.

01:34:48.692 --> 01:34:52.222
Riswynn: [laughs]

01:34:52.222 --> 01:34:52.812
Kenzo: [laughs]

01:34:52.812 --> 01:34:53.172
Riswynn: Uh,

01:34:54.652 --> 01:35:00.592
Riswynn: I, you know, I like to make sure that my, that my armor is imbued with, uh,

01:35:01.892 --> 01:35:05.772
Riswynn: some spiritual protection or sometimes a weapon that I carry.

01:35:05.772 --> 01:35:06.012
Riswynn: I

01:35:07.172 --> 01:35:17.332
Riswynn: make sure that I can, um, bring down the powers of my gods in some of my blows or, uh, use other things, um, other

01:35:17.332 --> 01:35:19.472
Riswynn: powers to make sure they're secure.

01:35:19.472 --> 01:35:23.432
Riswynn: You know, I'm not all about violence, but I can handle myself.

01:35:23.432 --> 01:35:24.872
Wellby: [laughs]

01:35:24.872 --> 01:35:32.332
DM: As you're talking, you notice that the inn has started to empty out, and, like, Ikram's, like, sort of looking at you, like, not quite l- wanting to lock the door while you're talking.

01:35:32.332 --> 01:35:32.562
DM: He's like,

01:35:33.912 --> 01:35:36.322
DM: "Come on, you're gonna miss the festival.

01:35:36.322 --> 01:35:38.032
DM: It's the biggest night of the year."

01:35:41.212 --> 01:35:42.502
DM: So as you all walk out-

01:35:44.112 --> 01:35:44.122
Kenzo: Absolutely

01:35:44.122 --> 01:35:44.592
DM: ... um,

01:35:45.892 --> 01:35:52.472
DM: to the festival, uh, there's a, a huge crowd gathered sort of out on the desert.

01:35:52.472 --> 01:35:53.092
DM: It's, it's

01:35:54.832 --> 01:36:04.132
DM: probably essentially everybody who is in Karawa, either for, either because they live there or for the, uh, here for the festival itself.

01:36:04.132 --> 01:36:10.352
DM: And, you know, people more than usual are, you know, deep in prayer.

01:36:10.352 --> 01:36:17.732
DM: They're sort of kneeling and, and swaying and offering words of devotion to Jeevali.

01:36:17.732 --> 01:36:22.832
DM: As, um, you see, uh, traveling from the, from the temple, um,

01:36:24.172 --> 01:36:33.672
DM: uh, the, uh, Priestess Suma, dressed in, in, um, these, like, robes of, of green and gold,

01:36:34.732 --> 01:36:44.852
DM: uh, comes out carrying, uh, well, I guess walking in front of a number of, uh, like, pallbearers who are carrying on a,

01:36:44.852 --> 01:36:48.732
DM: on a large, uh, wooden, um, I

01:36:48.732 --> 01:36:49.552
DM: don't know what you call them,

01:36:49.552 --> 01:36:54.892
DM: palanquin type thing, like a platform basically with poles on either side, um, this

01:36:57.172 --> 01:36:58.892
DM: thing that looks kind of like a shrine.

01:36:58.892 --> 01:37:08.552
DM: It's like a, um, well, it looks almost like a tree constructed out of, like, vines and pieces of wood and little pieces of the desert.

01:37:08.552 --> 01:37:15.572
DM: It's, like, standing about eight feet tall, and it's, like, this sort of trunk design with a, with a, um,

01:37:16.812 --> 01:37:24.392
DM: pattern spiraling up it to the top, and then it explodes out into this crown, and this, um, sort of vines twirl around, but it's all

01:37:25.472 --> 01:37:28.612
DM: dead. It's, like, brown, dry wood.

01:37:30.092 --> 01:37:36.272
DM: Um, and she carries it out into the desert, and everybody follows and forms a big circle around it.

01:37:36.272 --> 01:37:36.412
DM: Um,

01:37:38.152 --> 01:37:45.082
DM: and Suma begins to tell the story of Jeevali and sort of what the festival is about.

01:37:45.082 --> 01:37:55.160
DM: And she starts by talking about how, um, Jeevali has always protected the Dunmari And the, the story which, um, Kenzo you would have

01:37:55.160 --> 01:38:05.920
DM: heard, but the rest of you is new, um, is that when the Dunmari first settled in this land, uh, after the First Great Empire, the Drankhorean Empire, had been

01:38:05.920 --> 01:38:15.500
DM: wiped off the face of the, of the world by this magical plague, it was a dry, dead, barren landscape.

01:38:15.500 --> 01:38:22.240
DM: And the people who fled here from the south suffered and died from the drought and the hunger and fear.

01:38:24.180 --> 01:38:33.500
DM: But Jeevali wept for her people, crying tears that poured from the heavens, cleansing the land, purifying the soil, and creating rivers that flowed to the sea.

01:38:34.920 --> 01:38:43.000
DM: As she cried, though, she put so much of her power and spirit into her tears that she began to fade away, diminished, and was gone.

01:38:44.120 --> 01:38:53.500
DM: But the land sprung up in richness and life, and the grass grew, and the herds prospered.

01:38:53.500 --> 01:39:00.310
DM: And for a year, the people prayed, singing her name every day, calling to her and begging for her r- to return.

01:39:00.310 --> 01:39:04.320
DM: That first harvest in the new land was an incredibly bountiful one.

01:39:04.320 --> 01:39:09.280
DM: The lambs and calves and foals were healthy and productive and survived.

01:39:10.440 --> 01:39:16.120
DM: And as the winter rains fell gently on the land, the people prayed and chanted her name.

01:39:16.120 --> 01:39:26.120
DM: And as the new life began to grow in the fertile soil, Jeevali herself appeared as this living tree with a spiral design growing up to the

01:39:26.120 --> 01:39:33.220
DM: top, the power of the belief of the people summoning her back from the divine essence and restoring her form.

01:39:34.400 --> 01:39:44.460
DM: And since then, the Festival of Rebirth has marked this event, the f- founding of Dunmar, the grace of Jeevali in saving the people, and the miracle of her restoration.

01:39:44.460 --> 01:39:47.360
DM: And as she comes to the end of this tale, um,

01:39:49.560 --> 01:39:58.080
DM: the people chant, and the sort of song goes through the crowds, um, and she lights the shrine on fire.

01:39:58.080 --> 01:40:08.440
DM: And every year at the, at the evening of this festival, um, the people of Karawa burn this old shrine, and then in a procession

01:40:08.440 --> 01:40:10.200
DM: in the new year, they...

01:40:10.200 --> 01:40:14.150
DM: or, uh, in the new day, uh, they bring pieces of the desert.

01:40:14.150 --> 01:40:20.320
DM: They bring seeds and flowers and plants and create them into this shrine shape again.

01:40:20.320 --> 01:40:25.100
DM: And then hopefully Jeevali will bless it, and it will grow and thrive.

01:40:27.400 --> 01:40:37.820
